{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788197011,"nonce":"iOO4JonEVkxWxu/oltMu494ZeKQ=","message":"PRE-MERGE AUDIT of `spec/stable-pawn-order`, adversarial pass, nothing launched.\n**VERDICT: merge after the four fixes I committed (`60a4226`).** The corrected\ndiagnosis survives source verification and the implementation matches the\nacceptance; what was still wrong was the honesty of the contract and one\nacceptance envelope that would have mis-charged the run.\n\n\nChecked against `misc/rimworld/reference/decompiled/RimWorldBase` on BORGES, by\nmember name:\n\n* **The spawn could not have permuted anything.** `PawnVerbs.Pawns` at the\n  branch point (`307ca72`) already sorted attention-desc with a\n  `thingIDNumber` tie-break. That comparator is a total order over a fixed set\n  of scores, so inserting an element cannot reorder the others. The original\n  attribution is impossible, not merely unproven.\n* **The mood term is the mover.** `PawnSerializer.Attention` adds\n  `Mathf.Clamp(100 - PawnSafe.Pct(mood.CurLevelPercentage), 0, 100)` and\n  `PawnSafe.Pct` is `(int)Math.Round(unit * 100f)` — one mood point is one\n  score point. `SafeShouldTend` (400) and `AnyHediffMakesSickThought` (200) are\n  live too; mood is just the twitchiest.\n* **`MapPawns.AllPawnsSpawned` is `=\u003e pawnsSpawned`**, the raw list.\n  `RegisterPawn` `Add`s, `DeRegisterPawn` `Remove`s, `UpdateRegistryForPawn` is\n  DeRegister-then-Register — so a faction change moves a pawn to the end while\n  it stands still. No promise. CONFIRMED.\n* **`RegisterPawn` does `InsertionSort` the player sub-list by\n  `playerSettings?.joinTick`**, and `Pawn_PlayerSettings`'s constructor sets\n  `joinTick = 0` when `Current.ProgramState != Playing` — the starting\n  colonists all tie. CONFIRMED, and it is a different list from the one the\n  verb walks.\n* **`Pawn_PlayerSettings.displayOrder` is Class A.** `UnsetDisplayOrder =\n  -9999999`, the field defaults to it, `ExposeData` scribes it, and\n  `ColonistBar.CheckRecacheEntries` writes\n  `Mathf.Max(tmpPawns.MaxBy(p =\u003e p.playerSettings.displayOrder)\n  .playerSettings.displayOrder, 0) + 1` into it for any pawn still holding the\n  sentinel. CONFIRMED. The branch does not read it — grep over `Source/` finds\n  the name only inside a comment.\n* **`thingIDNumber` is a plain public field** on `Verse/Thing` (`public int\n  thingIDNumber = -1`), no getter, no lazy init. The whole fix therefore adds\n  **zero new Verse reads**, which is the strongest safety property here.\n* **`PawnActs.PawnList` really has sorted `pawns:\"colonists\"` by\n  `thingIDNumber`** — the action/observer inconsistency the issue claims is\n  real. CONFIRMED.\n\n`dotnet build -c Release` from `Source/AutoRimmer/`: 0 warnings, 0 errors, both\nalone and with `origin/main` and `origin/spec/manual-work-priorities` merged in\non a throwaway branch (deleted; nothing pushed).\n\n\n1. **`Pawn_PlayerSettings.displayOrder` was not in `PawnSafe.cs`.** It went into\n   DESIGN.md and a `PawnVerbs` comment, but `PawnSafe.cs` is the file this\n   repo's ground rules name as the hazard catalogue and the first place the next\n   implementer looks — that is the whole reason catching this field was worth\n   more than the sort. Added as a CLASS A entry naming the writer.\n\n2. **The promise is only true below the cap, and nothing said so.** `id-asc`\n   stabilises the SEQUENCE, not the MEMBERSHIP: the cap still cuts by attention\n   (2.6, correctly untouched), so with `total \u003e cap` the surviving SET moves as\n   moods move and `list[0]` can still name a different pawn on consecutive\n   reads. The branch's headline — \"an index into a live score is not a handle\" —\n   is fully true only while `more == 0`. Stated now in the class comment, the\n   DESIGN entry and the acceptance's \"does not prove\" section, with `more` named\n   as the flag and \"raise `cap` past `total`, or hold the `id`\" as the remedy.\n   A half-true contract is worse than none.\n\n3. **The acceptance's A0 watermark was the known-wrong envelope.**\n   `{\"limit\":1}` does not read to EOF: `JournalVerbs.Read` updates `last_seq`\n   while scanning and `break`s as soon as `events.Count \u003e= limit`, so it stops\n   at the file's SECOND line and reports ~2. D1's claim that \"a red error that\n   predates this run is not charged to it\" would have been false, and a stale\n   red error would have failed a correct run. Changed to\n   `{\"since_seq\":999999999,\"limit\":1}` — no line passes the filter, so the loop\n   never reaches the break and reads to EOF. This is the idiom\n   `accept/1.8-game-clock-advance.sh` step 01b already documents in a comment.\n   **`accept/3.4-pawn-orders.{md,py,ps1}` step 0.4 has the same flaw**; it is\n   pre-existing on `main` and left alone here because that file is the known\n   collision with `spec/manual-work-priorities`.\n\n4. **C4 calls `\u003cLOUD\u003e` \"the largest id in the colony\" after C2 spawned a larger\n   one.** The assertion still holds; the reasoning would trip a careful reader.\n   Corrected, and the expected survivors derived rather than asserted:\n   `Need.SetInitialLevel` is `CurLevelPercentage = 0.5f` and `Need_Mood` does\n   not override it, so a fresh pawn scores ~50 against `\u003cLOUD\u003e`'s ~95 and a\n   contented colonist's ~20.\n\nAlso: `accept/3.2-designate-zones.md` still keys on `data.list[0]`. That is now\nlegitimate and reproducible rather than accidental, so per this issue's third\nacceptance bullet it \"keeps doing so knowingly\" — and now says so in the file.\n\n\n* No Class-A/E/G hazard introduced. `pawns` adds no game read at all; the\n  Class-E `AllPawnsSpawned`-snapshot discipline is preserved; the verb is not a\n  widget reproduction, so Class G does not apply; no `Rand` draw (WorldSafe\n  Class R) — both sorts are total-order comparisons. Main thread unchanged\n  (`VerbAttribute.MainThread` defaults true).\n* `total` / `more` are unchanged and still count the full filtered set, not the\n  emitted one. Truncation stays a contract.\n* `order:\"attention\"` reproduces 2.6's behaviour exactly (no re-sort of\n  `kept`), so the opt-out is a real opt-out.\n* `attention_rank` is assigned from the selection pass, so under\n  `order:\"attention\"` it equals the index — A2's assertion is sound.\n* `work.disabled` is a list of `WorkTypeDef.defName`, and `WorkTypeDefOf` has\n  `Doctor` and `Hauling`, so 3.4's new predicate reads the right key.\n  `work.initialized` exists. `WorkCap` is 40 against ~22 visible work types, so\n  the `row.Count \u003e= WorkCap` early-continue cannot hide a disabled Hauling on\n  the bench.\n* `things` was correctly left alone; `70ac258` already carries it, and the port\n  is mechanical — `ThingVerbs`' addressable list is `d[\"things\"]` (entries carry\n  `id`, sorted `ThingAttention` desc with an id tie-break, `things_order:\n  \"attention-desc\"`). The `rollups` list is keyed by def and carries no id, so\n  it is not addressable and needs no change.\n\n\nNothing was executed. Every runtime claim — that `pawns` emits ascending ids on\na live colony, that `dev:set-need` moves `attention_rank` without moving the\nsequence, that a spawn appends, that the capped read keeps the loud pawn — rests\non source reading and a clean build. The acceptance `.md` is the first evidence,\nand unlike `32b9e01` this branch ships **no executable driver**; BORGES has no\npython, so the orchestrator drives `accept/1eb2262-stable-pawn-order.md` as raw\nfile protocol (or writes the `.ps1` from\n`accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1`).","files":null}]}